<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chaulukya dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 940–<a href="/years/1244">1244</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Solanki dynasty, Chaulukyas of Gujarat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chaulukya dynasty, also called the Solankis, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patan-gujarat">Anahilapataka</a> (Patan) from about 940 to 1244. Under kings such as Bhima I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayasimha-siddharaja">Jayasimha Siddharaja</a> and Kumarapala it controlled much of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and patronised Jain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples, including the sun temple at Modhera. Its feudatories included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chahamanas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalore">Jalor</a>, and its power passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghel">Vaghelas</a>, a collateral branch.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chaulukya-dynasty.jpg" alt="Chaulukya dynasty" width="320" height="296" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Map created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan. Reference: [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Chaulukyas.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The dynasty called itself Chaulukya in almost all its records; Solanki is the vernacular form of the name. The word is thought to be a variant of Chalukya, but unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of Kalyani and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> house never claimed descent from the Chalukyas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Vatapi</a>. It shared their myth that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a> created the founding hero from his chuluka, a pot or cupped palm filled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> water, a story of no historical value. Colonial-era historians read the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agnikula">Agnikula</a> fire-pit legend as a sign of foreign Gurjara origin, but the Chaulukyas themselves linked that legend to the Paramaras, and the theory has been weakened.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Chaulukyas rose as the Gurjara-Pratihara and Rashtrakuta empires declined. Their founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mularaja">Mularaja</a> supplanted Samantasimha, the last Chavda king, in the mid-10th century, and his successors fought the Chudasamas, the Paramaras and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chahamanas</a> of Shakambhari. In 1024–1025 the Ghaznavid ruler Mahmud entered the kingdom unopposed and sacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somnath-temple">Somnath temple</a> while Bhima I fled to Kanthkot; Bhima restored his rule afterwards and, by accounts that are partly legend, joined the Kalachuri king Lakshmi-Karna in bringing down the Paramara king Bhoja around 1055.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayasimha-siddharaja">Jayasimha Siddharaja</a> (r. c. 1092–1142) annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> in 1135–1136 and pushed east to the Chandela kingdom. Under his relative Kumarapala the realm stretched from Chittor and Jaisalmer to the Vindhyas and the Tapti river, with Kachchha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a>. In 1178 a Chaulukya army defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-of-ghor">Muhammad of Ghor</a> at Kasahrada. Under the young Bhima II provincial governors rebelled, and on 4 February 1197 Qutb al-Din Aibak dealt the Chaulukyas a heavy defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patan-gujarat">Anahilapataka</a>. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghel">Vaghela</a> generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavanaprasada">Lavanaprasada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viradhavala">Viradhavala</a> saved the kingdom more than once and took royal titles, and after Tribhuvanapala they seized the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Māru-Gurjara or Chaulukya style of temple architecture arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> under the dynasty in the 11th to 13th centuries and, mainly through Jain patronage, later spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Most of the rulers were Shaivaite but also patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>: the earliest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilwara-temples">Dilwara Temples</a> were built under Bhima I, whose queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udayamati">Udayamati</a> is traditionally credited with the Queen's step-well, and Jain accounts remember Kumarapala as the last great royal patron of their faith. The kings also endowed mosques to keep good relations with Muslim traders. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghel">Vaghelas</a> claimed descent from a sister of Kumarapala, and Solanki princely houses such as the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunavada">Lunavada</a> claimed descent from the dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chaulukya dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He captured Multan in 1175 but was defeated by the <b>Chaulukyas of Gujarat</b> at Kasahrada in 1178, and he ended Ghaznavid rule in the Punjab by taking Lahore in 1186.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Over the following decades the Chandelas took the great fortress of Kalanjara, the Paramaras established themselves in Malwa, the <b>Chaulukyas</b> in Gujarat and the Kalachuris in central India, and further Rashtrakuta campaigns under Krishna III weakened the north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chalukyas also fought the Paramaras of Malwa, the Kalachuris of Tripuri and the <b>Chaulukyas of Gujarat</b> in the north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The rulers of Vansda (Bansda) were Solanki Rajputs whose line claimed descent from the <b>Chaulukya</b> kings of Gujarat.</blockquote><a href="/state/bansda-state">Bansda State</a></li><li><blockquote>The kings held a strong fort controlling routes between Gujarat and the Indo-Gangetic plain, and acted at times as allies or vassals of the <b>Chaulukyas of Gujarat</b> and later the Delhi Sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/chahamanas-of-jalor">Chahamanas of Jalor</a></li><li><blockquote>They fought the Paramara dynasty and the Chaulukya dynasty, and from the 12th century often served the <b>Chaulukyas</b> as feudatories; Alhana and his son Kelhana fought in Chaulukya campaigns.</blockquote><a href="/state/chahamanas-of-naddula">Chahamanas of Naddula</a></li></ul></section>
